---
/**
 * /laya — the topic cover for the open-weight decision model.
 *
 * This is the second model topic on a site that was built around one, so it is
 * also the test of whether the shape generalises: a cover page that says what the
 * thing is, and columns underneath it that answer one question each. Laya's
 * columns are not Jev's — its questions are different (can I run it, can I ship
 * it, what do the weights license) — which is what the topic-as-namespace
 * decision was for.
 *
 * Facts live in lib/laya.ts, our own numbers in lib/laya-our-measurements.ts. The
 * split is deliberate: a reader can tell which sentences are the publisher's,
 * which are ours, and which are Jev's.
 */
import System from "../../../layouts/System.astro";
import Breadcrumbs from "../../../components/Breadcrumbs.astro";
import JsonLd from "../../../components/JsonLd.astro";
import Callout from "../../../components/Callout.astro";
import FactTable from "../../../components/FactTable.astro";
import Provenance from "../../../components/Provenance.astro";
import SubscribeForm from "../../../components/SubscribeForm.astro";
import { articleSchema, SITE, SITE_NAME } from "../../../lib/schema";
import { checkpoints, ecosystem, LAYA_GITHUB_URL, LAYA_HF_URL, LAYA_LICENSE, LAYA_PACKAGE_VERSION, LAYA_PYPI_URL, LAYA_READ_ON, trainingConfig } from "@repo/lib/laya";
import { LAYA_OURS_MEASURED_ON, environment, latencyCpu, schemaProbe } from "@repo/lib/laya-our-measurements";

const title = "Laya, Specified: Checkpoints, Limits and What We Measured (2026)";
const h1 = "Laya, specified";
const description =
  "Laya is a 421M open-weight decision model: a state and typed questions in, one forward pass out, Apache-2.0, and a server that answers TypeSafe's `/v1/systemone`. What it is, what we measured on a CPU, and where the model card says it fails.";
const canonical = `${SITE}/laya/reference`;

const specs = [
  { label: "Maker", value: "Convai Innovations", note: "Author: Nandhakishor, publishing as Convai Innovations." },
  { label: "Licence", value: LAYA_LICENSE, note: "Read from the model card's frontmatter; the card also tags it `commercial-use`." },
  { label: "Class", value: "System One decision model", note: "Returns typed answers with probabilities; it does not generate text." },
  { label: "Question types", value: "choice · score · noul", note: "The same three TypeSafe uses, which is what makes the protocol claim possible." },
  { label: "English checkpoint", value: "ModernBERT-large, 421M parameters", note: "512-token window, of which roughly 320 is left for the state." },
  { label: "Package", value: `laya ${LAYA_PACKAGE_VERSION}`, note: `On ${LAYA_READ_ON} that was the current release.` },
  { label: "Hosted price", value: "None — you run it", note: "The cost is your machine, your electricity and your weekend." },
  { label: "Weights", value: "2.3 GB for all three checkpoints", note: "843 MB for the English one alone — the encoder weights are inside that file, not a separate download." },
];

const columns = [
  { href: "/laya/vs-jev", label: "Laya vs Jev", blurb: "The same thirteen adversarial cases through both models, and the published benchmarks with whose ruler each number came from." },
  { href: "/laya/self-hosting", label: "Self-hosting", blurb: "What it takes to run it: CPU latency we measured, the token budgets, the router, and the two things that break." },
  { href: "/laya/open-weights", label: "Open weights", blurb: "What Apache-2.0 covers here, what the three checkpoints are, and what the licence does not reach." },
];
---

<System title={`${title} | ${SITE_NAME}`} description={description} canonical={canonical} ogType="article">
  <JsonLd slot="head" data={articleSchema({ title, description, publishedAt: LAYA_READ_ON, updatedAt: LAYA_OURS_MEASURED_ON, url: canonical })} />

  <Breadcrumbs schema={true} items={[{ name: "Laya", url: `${SITE}/laya` },
    { name: "Reference", url: canonical }]} />

  <article class="col">
    <header class="page-head">
      <p class="card-eyebrow">Model reference · verified</p>
      <h1>{h1}</h1>
      <p class="lede">
        Laya is an open-weight decision model from Convai Innovations: give it a state — a ticket,
        an email, a JSON blob — and a set of typed questions, and one forward pass returns typed
        answers with probabilities. It never writes text, and the three question types it accepts
        are the ones TypeSafe's Jev accepts, which is why its server can stand in front of a client
        written for Jev.
      </p>

      {/* The topic cover, in the same slot the Jev topic uses (contract §5): one image
          about this page's subject, meaning in the alt text, no caption announcing it. */}
      <figure class="figure cover">
        <img src="/img/cover-laya-topic.webp" alt="An engraving: a small brass instrument with three dials on a single base, a blank paper tag tied to it." width="1024" height="576" decoding="async" />
      </figure>
    </header>

    <Callout eyebrow="In short">
      <li><strong>421M parameters, Apache-2.0, no key.</strong> The English checkpoint is a ModernBERT-large with a two-layer head; the card also ships a 322M multilingual one and a 421M checkpoint tuned for four decision workflows.</li>
      <li><strong>It runs on a laptop.</strong> A single question cost 0.30 s on a 2018 CPU; ten took 2.09 s and fifty took 10.4 s, about 0.21 s each.</li>
      <li><strong>It claims to be drop-in for Jev, and the claim mostly holds.</strong> Our Jev client, pointed at its server, returned a valid answer for all thirteen of our adversarial cases.</li>
      <li><strong>On the same thirteen cases it agreed with Jev eleven times</strong> — two different models, two different training stories, the same labels on most of the set.</li>
      <li><strong>The card is unusually straight about the failures</strong>: near-chance zero-shot on its own hard benchmark, a per-option token budget that breaks large option sets, and an ordinal `score` primitive it calls the weakest of the three.</li>
    </Callout>

    <section>
      <h2>The facts</h2>
      <FactTable rows={specs} />
      <Provenance
        source="huggingface.co/convaiinnovations/laya"
        verified={`model card and shipped files, ${LAYA_READ_ON}`}
        extra={[
          `GitHub repository created ${ecosystem.created}; ${ecosystem.commits} commits from ${ecosystem.contributors} contributors, read 2026-09-23.`,
          "We downloaded the English checkpoint and its encoder and ran everything on this page ourselves; the runner is in tools/laya-lab/.",
        ]}
      />
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>Three checkpoints, one interface</h2>
      <p>
        The family is small and the differences are the kind that change an integration: the
        window, the language coverage, and how much of that window is left for the state once the
        options have taken their share.
      </p>
      <div class="table-scroll">
        <table class="data">
          <thead>
            <tr>
              <th>Checkpoint</th>
              <th>Backbone</th>
              <th class="num">Params</th>
              <th class="num">Context</th>
              <th class="num">Left for the state</th>
              <th>Best at</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            {checkpoints.map((row) => (
              <tr>
                <td><code>{row.name}</code></td>
                <td>{row.backbone}</td>
                <td class="num">{row.params}</td>
                <td class="num">{row.context}</td>
                <td class="num">{row.stateBudget}</td>
                <td>{row.bestAt}</td>
              </tr>
            ))}
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      <p>
        That fourth column is the one people miss. A 512-token window sounds like twice a 256-token
        one; in practice the options are rendered into the same window, so the English checkpoint
        leaves about 320 tokens for the ticket itself and the rest is option text. A long support
        thread is truncated sooner than the number suggests — and the model will still answer.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>What we ran it on, and what came back</h2>
      <p>
        Everything here ran on the machine this site is written on — an i7-8550U with no
        accelerator — after a download of {environment.weights}. The four numbers that matter to
        somebody deciding whether to try it:
      </p>
      <div class="table-scroll">
        <table class="data">
          <thead>
            <tr><th>What we did</th><th class="num">Result</th><th>How it compares</th></tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr>
              <td>20 identical calls, three questions each</td>
              <td class="num">range 0.0000</td>
              <td>Every signal identical to four decimals. Jev's label was equally stable but its confidence moved 0.38–0.58.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>13 adversarial cases × 4 runs</td>
              <td class="num">0 off-menu</td>
              <td>No answer ever left the option set the caller defined — the same result we got from Jev.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>Our Jev client, pointed at its server</td>
              <td class="num">13 / 13 parsed</td>
              <td>{schemaProbe.dropIn.parseable}</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>50 questions in one call</td>
              <td class="num">10.4 s</td>
              <td>0.21 s per question, flat from ten questions up.</td>
            </tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      <Provenance
        source="tools/laya-lab/laya-probe.py"
        verified={LAYA_OURS_MEASURED_ON}
        extra={[`Runs on ${environment.machine}, ${environment.memory} of RAM, no GPU.`]}
      />
      <p>
        The measurement that made us write this section rather than quote the card: on the
        adversarial set, Laya and Jev returned the <strong>same label on eleven of thirteen</strong>
        cases. Two models that share no weights and no training data agreeing that often on
        deliberately strange inputs is the most interesting thing in this comparison, and it is on{" "}
        <a href="/laya/vs-jev">the head-to-head page</a> case by case.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>The column of things it cannot do</h2>
      <p>
        Most of this comes from the model card's own "Honest Limits" section, which is longer and
        blunter than most vendors write. One entry is ours, and it is the reason the{" "}
        <a href="/laya/self-hosting">self-hosting page</a> exists.
      </p>
      <div class="table-scroll">
        <table class="data">
          <thead>
            <tr><th>Limit</th><th>Whose number</th><th>What it costs you</th></tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr>
              <td>Zero-shot, the base checkpoints are near chance on typed-decisions (0.362 against a 0.461 majority baseline)</td>
              <td>the card's</td>
              <td>Treat it as a fast thing to fine-tune, not a decision engine you point at your data on day one.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>Options share a fixed token budget, so 77 options get three or four tokens each — 0.425 on Banking77, where Jev scores 0.870</td>
              <td>the card's</td>
              <td>Under 20 options is the comfortable zone. Beyond that you are tuning `head_max_len` or splitting the choice in two.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>An ordinal <code>score</code> question is the weakest primitive (0.372 on SST-5)</td>
              <td>the card's</td>
              <td>Severity scales drift with the wording — we measured 0.47 of a level between two phrasings of the same scale.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td><code>noul</code> can follow its two level labels instead of the state</td>
              <td>the card's, reproduced by us</td>
              <td>On our sentiment question the English checkpoint returned 0.000 at confidence 1.0 for five out of five reviews, three of them glowing.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>The shipped <code>choice:11+</code> temperature is outside the range its own library accepts</td>
              <td>ours</td>
              <td>Every choice question with eleven or more options gets a confidence the library itself calls uncalibrated.</td>
            </tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>Where to go next</h2>
      <div class="cards three">
        {columns.map((column) => (
          <a class="card" href={column.href}>
            <span class="card-title">{column.label}</span>
            <span class="card-blurb">{column.blurb}</span>
          </a>
        ))}
      </div>
      <p class="form-note">
        Source material: <a href={LAYA_GITHUB_URL}>the repository</a> ·{" "}
        <a href={LAYA_HF_URL}>the model card</a> · <a href={LAYA_PYPI_URL}>the package</a>.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>What this page cannot tell you</h2>
      <p>
        It cannot tell you whether Laya is good at your task. Our tests are thirteen adversarial
        cases, forty ticket-labelling calls and a handful of option-set experiments — enough to say
        where the sharp edges are, not enough to rank two models. It cannot tell you what it costs
        at scale, because the honest answer depends on the machine you were going to buy anyway. And
        it cannot verify the publisher's benchmark table: those runs happened on their hardware
        against their test sets, and the Jev column in it was never measured by them at all.
      </p>
      <p class="quoted">
        The one number we could have argued about is the star count — {ecosystem.stars.toLocaleString("en-US")} on {LAYA_READ_ON}, up from 19,196 the day before —
        and we could not corroborate it: GitHub's star-history endpoint answers 404 for this
        repository, so it is printed as the API reported it and no more.
      </p>
    </section>

    <SubscribeForm />
  </article>
</System>

<style>
  .cards.three { display: grid; grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(220px, 1fr)); gap: var(--space-4); margin: var(--space-4) 0; }
  .card { display: block; text-decoration: none; }
  .card-title { display: block; font-family: var(--sans); font-weight: 600; color: var(--text); margin-bottom: var(--space-2); }
  .card-blurb { display: block; font-size: var(--text-sm); color: var(--text-muted); }
</style>
